  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Multiple chances for thunderstorms today. Storms\nhave the potential to be strong/severe.\n\nA potent upper shortwave will traverse through the Great Lakes\nthis morning, shifting into New England by this evening. Slight\namplification of this wave will occur by the afternoon, and\nmultiple waves of storms will be possible. \n\nCurrent satellite indicates a decaying Mesoscale Convective\nSystem over the Tennessee Valley with some renewed development\nnow across the upper Ohio Valley. Guidance has development \nwithin this zone continuing over the next few hours, but \nconfidence in this is still rather uncertain given meager \ncoverage of storms ongoing when compared to model simulated \nreflectivity. Should this area fill in as modeled, it will drop\nsouth into the forecast area around daybreak. Severe threat \nwith this should be fairly minimal based on limited \ninstability, however increasing bulk shear could support a few \norganized bowing segments capable of strong wind gusts. \n\nA second round of storms will be possible this afternoon\nassociated with the main frontal passage. Storms this afternoon\nwill be highly dependent on how the morning convection\nprogresses through the area. At the moment, it appears there\nwill at least be some clearing by early afternoon resulting in\nmarginal surface heating within a moist boundary layer yielding\nMLCAPE around 1000 to 1500 J/kg. Increasing deep layer shear\noverlapping with favorable instability will likely result in\nwidely scattered storms capable of all severe modes. 0-6 km\nshear (30-40 knots) is also supportive of supercells, which \ncould produce isolated tornadoes given increasing 0-3 km SRH by\nmid- afternoon. Supercells and organized multicell line \nsegments will also be capable of severe wind gusts.\n\nA localized flash flooding threat also exists within areas of\ntraining/repeated rounds of storms. Overall, storms motions\nshould help mitigate the flooding threat today.\n\nThe front and trough axis moves quickly southeast and out of the \nforecast area by midnight. Upslope flow may bring a few showers\nto the mountains overnight, but the severe threat will be over.",
